// --no-sysroot-includes (tests/testnosysrootinc.flags): the toolchain-only view
// of the system include table — the C++ stdlib dirs and the compiler's builtin
// dir stay, the host's C library / SDK dirs do not. <sys/sysinfo.h> exists
// only there, so this #include must fail LOUDLY (compile refused, the name in
// the diagnostic). Positive control: tests/testsysrootinc.mad, the same program
// without the flag. This is the FREEZER's posture (scripts/forest_pack_darwin.sh):
// a frozen forest is the header-less consumer's corpus, and a build host that
// carries a real SDK must not leak its text into the groves.
// No <stdio.h>: on linux madc's embedded C headers are a SUPPLEMENT, not a
// provider (glibc's own header is the one that resolves), so under the flag
// stdio.h would fail first and mask the header this pair is about. The
// always-available println intrinsic keeps the program include-free apart
// from the sysroot-only header.
#include <sys/sysinfo.h>
int main()
{
	struct sysinfo s;
	int ok = sysinfo(&s) == 0;
	println("sysroot header resolved: {}", ok);
	return 0;
}
